    bio: "Anna Comnena was born in the purple-walled Porphyra chamber of the imperial palace in Constantinople, the eldest child of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos, and she never quite recovered from the grandeur of her origins. From childhood she was educated to a standard extraordinary for any person of her era, let alone a woman: she studied Greek philosophy, rhetoric, medicine, history, and astronomy under the finest scholars the Byzantine court could provide. She read Homer and Aristotle with the same appetite, and her writing would later display a command of classical Greek so precise that she would be read with admiration by scholars a thousand years after her death.\n\nShe was betrothed in infancy to the young prince Constantine Doukas, who was expected to inherit the throne, and for a brief period she was herself heir apparent. But when her brother John was born and Alexios reshuffled his dynastic priorities, Anna\'s position shifted. She married the historian and general Nikephoros Bryennios, whom she loved deeply and who shared her intellectual passions, and she devoted herself to scholarship and court life while nursing a barely concealed fury at having been denied the empire she believed was rightfully hers. After her father died in 1118 and her brother John II became emperor, she was implicated in a plot to place her husband on the throne. When it failed — reportedly because Nikephoros refused to participate — she was stripped of her estates and retired to a monastery.\n\nIt was in that monastery, with decades of enforced distance from the court she had inhabited since birth, that Anna produced the work that would make her immortal. The Alexiad is a history of her father\'s reign in fifteen books, written in a self-consciously elevated Greek prose that deliberately echoes Thucydides and the great classical historians. It is the only major medieval historical work written by a woman, and it is a primary source of enormous value for the First Crusade — Anna watched the Crusader armies pass through Constantinople as a child, and her portrait of their leaders, particularly Bohemond of Taranto, combines fascination with barely disguised contempt. She describes Bohemond as startlingly handsome and utterly dangerous, a barbarian dressed in the armour of a knight.\n\nAnna Comnena died around 1153, probably in the monastery where she had spent her final decades. The Alexiad was not widely read in the West until the seventeenth century, when a Latin translation made her extraordinary voice accessible beyond the Greek-speaking world. She had written, in her preface, that grief was her reason for writing — grief for her husband, her father, and the life she had been denied. What she produced instead was a monument: the most vivid, personal, and intellectually ambitious history to survive from the entire Byzantine millennium."
